WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.080
Data science and data analytics. Are they the 
same thing? Well, you may have seen these terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:15.240
used interchangeably, but if I'm mining data from&nbsp;
a large dataset, am I performing data science or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.840
am I performing data analytics? Or what if I'm&nbsp;
trying to create a prediction of when my store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:27.680
will sell out of our current inventory of&nbsp;
cantaloupes? Well, is that analytics or is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.760
that science? It's worthwhile to understand the&nbsp;
difference to better comprehend what these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:40.200
fields can do, and also, if you're considering&nbsp;
a career in either field. After all, these are two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:49.360
different jobs. Somebody who works in the field&nbsp;
of data science is known as a data scientist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:59.440
For data analytics, that role is called a data&nbsp;
analyst. Now, this is kind of a trick question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.120
because we can classify everything-- data&nbsp;
mining, data forecasting and all the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:12.320
it --as simply data science. And that's because&nbsp;
data science is the overarching umbrella term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.560
that covers tasks related to finding patterns in&nbsp;
large datasets, training machine learning models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:26.880
and deploying AI applications. Data analytics, it&nbsp;
could be argued, is one task that resides under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:34.040
the data science umbrella. It's a specialization&nbsp;
of data science, and it focuses on querying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:41.760
interpreting and visualizing datasets. Data&nbsp;
science is iterative, meaning data scientists form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.520
hypotheses and experiments to see if a desired&nbsp;
outcome can be achieved using available data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:56.960
And that is a process that is known as the data&nbsp;
science lifecycle, which usually follows seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:06.360
phases. So first is to identify a problem or an&nbsp;
opportunity. Then the next phase is data mining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.640
which is to extract data relevant to that problem&nbsp;
or opportunity from large datasets. Now that data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.880
will likely consist of a bunch of redundancies&nbsp;
and errors, which is fixed in the next stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:26.920
data cleaning. And then at that point, we move on&nbsp;
to data exploration analysis to try to make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:34.360
of that data. We'll then apply feature engineering&nbsp;
using domain knowledge to extract details from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:42.240
data. And predictive modeling comes next to use&nbsp;
the data to predict or forecast future outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:49.040
and behaviors. And then finally, we have data&nbsp;
visualization that represents the data points with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.400
graphical tools such as charts and animations.&nbsp;
And so the lifecycle repeats. Now, the role of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.760
data scientist is an in-demand profession right&nbsp;
now. If that's something you're interested in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.920
you'll want to develop deep skills in machine&nbsp;
learning and AI. It's helpful to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:14.080
write code in languages such as Python--also in R--&nbsp;
it's another popular language for data science. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:22.600
you should have experience working with big data&nbsp;
platforms. So perhaps Hadoop or Apache Spark. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:30.440
it's also very helpful to have database knowledge&nbsp;
and SQL. So that's data science. But what about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:39.160
its specialization, data analytics? Well, the job&nbsp;
of a data analyst is to conceptualize a data set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:48.320
as it currently exists. So we have some data&nbsp;
here and we need to do something with it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.840
we need to be able to make decisions based on&nbsp;
this data. How do we conceptualize it? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:00.600
four ways. One is through predictive analytics,&nbsp;
which helps to identify trends, correlations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.880
causation within datasets like forecasting when&nbsp;
those cantaloupes would have all flown off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:13.680
shelves. Or, in health care, to forecast regions&nbsp;
which will experience a rise in flu cases. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.760
prescriptive analytics, and that predicts likely&nbsp;
outcomes and makes decision recommendations like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:26.200
predicting when a tire will wear out and need&nbsp;
to be replaced. There's diagnostic analytics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:31.880
that helps pinpoint the reason an event occurred.&nbsp;
Manufacturers can analyze a failed component on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:38.640
assembly line and figure out the reason behind its&nbsp;
failure. And then there is descriptive analytics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.120
which evaluates the qualities and quantities of&nbsp;
a data set. A content streaming provider might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:49.360
use descriptive analytics to understand how many&nbsp;
subscribers it's lost or how many it's gained over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:56.800
a given period of time and what content is being&nbsp;
watched. And while a data scientist is a clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:03.840
defined and specialized role, virtually any&nbsp;
stakeholder can be a data analyst. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:10.280
business analysts can use BI dashboards to conduct&nbsp;
business analytics and visualize KPIs. But many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:16.960
organizations do employ professional, dedicated&nbsp;
data analysts, responsible for data wrangling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:22.440
and interpreting findings like why a company's&nbsp;
marketing campaign didn't meet expectations. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:29.560
you want to be a data analyst, it helps to have&nbsp;
both analytical and programming skills. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:38.920
includes familiarity with databases. Also, you'll&nbsp;
need to know about statistical analysis. And also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:47.480
data visualization is another important skill.&nbsp;
So data analytics is often more focused on using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:53.560
statistical tools and techniques to interpret&nbsp;
existing data and offer actionable insights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:58.200
It's usually less concerned with creating&nbsp;
new algorithms or models. Data science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.480
on the other hand, has a broader scope that can&nbsp;
involve complex machine learning algorithms, often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:09.400
created from scratch. Data science focuses&nbsp;
on phases from data collection to predictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:15.720
modeling. Data analysis, on the other hand, is&nbsp;
more about answering specific questions with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:22.480
that data. And if you've done both your&nbsp;
data science and data analytics right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:29.400
you'll always be able to keep cantaloupes&nbsp;
and just about everything else in stock.

